 "action_summary": "The owner asked again why the machine cannot be given the most advanced coding actions, once each, and then have them for good. The repository already owns the mechanism for that: canonical/swe_kernel/verified_skill_crystallizer_v1.py (VerifiedSkillStore), reached in production through canonical/swe_kernel/retained_repair_session_v1.py. So it was given the experiences instead of an argument: real single-file commits from public projects, one at a time in history order, each graded against the maintainers' own result. Two defects in owned code came out of that. (1) The query that feeds recall ended in LIMIT 128, ordered by reuse count: once 128 lessons had each been reused, every later lesson was stored, reported crystallized, and never read again; with no reuse the oldest lessons dropped out instead. (2) The harvest that runs a project's tests inside the solver called setUp and never tearDown, so a per-test alarm stayed armed in the solver. This change removes the cut, keeps the order, and ends each harvested test the way unittest ends it. It does not make a lesson help on a problem it was not taught on. That was measured too and is unchanged: of the 549 real code changes in the last 600 single-file commits of psf/requests, none could be produced from the earlier lessons before it was shown, on the base commit and on this one alike; 191 of the 549 could be kept as a lesson at all. The honest reading of highest_leverage_now, which the admission gate requires to be true: this is the highest-leverage change that is certain today on the question the owner asked; the larger lever is what the repair search can reach and how applicable lessons are ranked, and it is named under next_step, not claimed here.",
